# Group Charter
## World Domination

1. Availability for meeting expectations
  1.1  All team members are expected to attend scheduled meetings and participate in project discussions.
  1.2  Meeting times will be determined based on everyone's availability.
  1.3  If a member cannot attend a meeting, they should notify the group as soon as possible.
  1.4  Members who miss a meeting are responsible for reviewing what was discussed and completing any assigned work.

2. Response time to communication expectations
  2.1 Responses to communication should be expected within a 24hr period unless there is reason/said group member is excused.

3. Definition of done: when is a task complete? (hint: "I'm done with that but I didn't check it in to github" is not done.)
Defention of completed work(done) : 

A task is complete when : 

Code is Written, Tested, and Committed: All code for the task is written, unit tests have been created and successfully passed, and the changes are committed and pushed to the main or feature branch on GitHub.

Code Review Approved: The pull request has been reviewed by the assigned memeber's of the team, commented on,and any requested changes have been addressed by the team

Merged without Conflicts: The branch has been successfully merged into the integration/main branch with no merge conflicts or build failures due to the newly merged work 

Documentation : Any relevant code comments, API documentation, or task boards (like Trello, Jira, or GitHub Projects) are fully updated to reflect the current state of the process.

(if any of these requirement are not fullfilled to the standard of the charter, member will be asked to completed the task to be considered "done" with a particular task)

  
4. Scrum meeting format/frequency and distribution of documentation tasks (meeting minutes, updating task board)

Frequency:
-   Weekly meeting: in person every Tuesday during lab time, lasting at
  most 1 hour 30 minutes.
-   Sprints: two weeks long. Sprint planning happens at the Tuesday meeting
  that starts a sprint, and the sprint review and retrospective happen at the
  Tuesday meeting that ends one. The Tuesday in the middle of a sprint is a
  regular check-in.
-   Stand-ups: async, posted in our group chat by 9 PM every Thursday and
  Sunday.

Stand-up format:
Each member posts answers to three questions:
1. What did I finish since my last update?
2. What am I working on next?
3. Is anything blocking me?
If someone reports a blocker, the people who can help reply in the chat or
meet up before the next Tuesday. Blockers don't wait for the weekly meeting.

Tuesday meeting format
-   Sprint start (planning):   move items from the Product Backlog to the
  Sprint Backlog, break them into issues, and assign an owner to each one.
-   Mid-sprint check-in:   go over the board, discuss blockers from the
  stand-ups, and adjust assignments if someone is overloaded.
-   Sprint end (review and retrospective):   show what reached Done, then
  discuss what went well and one thing to improve next sprint. Unfinished
  items go back to the Product Backlog or carry into the next sprint.

  Documentation roles-   (rotate every sprint so everyone does each role)
-   Scrum Master: runs the Tuesday meeting, keeps it within 1 hour 30
  minutes, and checks in on anyone who reported a blocker in a stand-up.
-   Note taker: records the minutes (attendance, decisions, task
  assignments, blockers) and commits them to the repo as
  `meetings/YYYY-MM-DD.md` within 24 hours of the meeting.
-   Board keeper: after each Tuesday meeting, makes sure the project board
  matches what was decided (new issues created, Sprint Backlog updated).
-   Everyone: moves their own cards on the board as their work progresses
  and posts their stand-ups on time.

  Role assignments: TBD
Roles will be assigned at our next lab meeting, and this section will be updated with the rotation schedule.

